Two faults are in play at once: the destination package declares error, shadowing
a predeclared identifier, and set init would generate a func init with a non-empty
signature (a reserved-name fault). The destination shadow is a whole-package
property, checked once before any set is solved, so it is reported first, ahead of
the set-scoped reserved-name fault that only surfaces while solving init. This pins
that precedence.
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
output: plumb_gen.go
error-is: ErrDestShadowsPredeclared
-- example.com/app/a.go --
package app

type error int

type Server struct{}

//plumb:init
func NewServer() *Server { return &Server{} }
